  1016                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1017                              <1> ;  Console layer
  1018                              <1> ;
  1019                              <1> ;  putc prints AL. waitk is the host's key routine: it waits up to EAX ms
  1020                              <1> ;  (0: just a look; -1: for ever) for a key, and returns it in AL with the
  1021                              <1> ;  zero flag clear, or AL = 0 with the zero flag set if none came. Both keep
  1022                              <1> ;  every register but EAX (putc keeps EAX too).
  1023                              <1> ;
  1024                              <1> ;  A running program reads the keyboard only through getk and pollk, which
  1025                              <1> ;  between them give the break key its rules:
  1026                              <1> ;    - Ctrl-C stops the program;
  1027                              <1> ;    - any other key that the break check reads is held at HELD for the
  1028                              <1> ;      next '?', and a newer one takes its place;
  1029                              <1> ;    - '?' (getk) takes the held key first;
  1030                              <1> ;    - a program that has stored a non-zero byte at BRKOFF gets Ctrl-C as a
  1031                              <1> ;      key like any other, but two of them within about 250 ms still stop it.
  1032                              <1> ;  Both are called straight from a handler (or from rpoll), never from a
  1033                              <1> ;  routine under one: the checked build's report counts on finding one
  1034                              <1> ;  return address above the data stack.
  1035                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1036                              <1> 
  1037                              <1> ; putc: WriteFile to the output handle (standard output; the checked
  1038                              <1> ; report switches it to standard error), one byte. PUSHAD saves everything;
  1039                              <1> ; the byte is AL in the saved EAX, and the count goes in the slot of the
  1040                              <1> ; saved ESP, which POPAD ignores.
  1041 000004A0 60                  <1> putc:   pushad
  1042 000004A1 8D4C240C            <1>         lea     ecx,[esp+12]
  1043 000004A5 8D54241C            <1>         lea     edx,[esp+28]
  1044 000004A9 6A00                <1>         push    0                       ; lpOverlapped
  1045 000004AB 51                  <1>         push    ecx                     ; lpNumberOfBytesWritten
  1046 000004AC 6A01                <1>         push    1
  1047 000004AE 52                  <1>         push    edx
  1048 000004AF FF7552              <1>         push    dword [V(v_outh)]
